{"acceptedResponses":["E"],"answer":"E","bank":"amc","batch":"NA","choiceOrder":["A","B","C","D","E"],"choices":{"A":"\\(2\\)","B":"\\(3\\)","C":"\\(6\\)","D":"\\(8\\)","E":"\\(9\\)"},"choicesType":"text","domain":[],"editorialNote":"","figureSources":{},"id":"2001_AMC_10_Q06","knowledgeGraph":{"assessedIds":["cnm_p017","cnm_027"],"contentHash":"69a7aee637c38576c63f8db54ede212ec5104d2fd9547981e3c693476ba85c47","flags":[],"reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"cancel-nonzero-tens-digit","label":"Cancel the tens digit","methodDirectNodeIds":{"cancel-nonzero-tens-digit":["cnm_040"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_040"]},{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"factor-digit-relation","label":"Factor the digit relation","methodDirectNodeIds":{"factor-digit-relation":["cnm_151"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_151"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_039"],"id":"model-tens-and-units","label":"Use tens and units","methodDirectNodeIds":{"model-tens-and-units":["cnm_p017","cnm_027","cnm_040"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_p017","cnm_027","cnm_040","cnm_039"]}],"status":"mapped","supportIds":["cnm_039"],"version":"1.0.0"},"level":"AMC 10","number":6,"question":"Let \\(P(n)\\) and \\(S(n)\\) denote the product and the sum, respectively, of the digits of the integer \\(n.\\) For example, \\(P(23)=6\\) and \\(S(23)=5.\\) Suppose \\(N\\) is a two-digit number such that \\(N=P(N)+S(N).\\) What is the units digit of \\(N?\\)","questionType":"text","rejectedAnswers":[],"responseType":"choice","schemaVersion":"3.0","skill":[],"solution":"Let \\(a\\) be the tens digit and \\(b\\) the units digit. Then \\(1\\le a\\le9\\), \\(0\\le b\\le9\\), and\n\\[\nN=10a+b,\\qquad P(N)=ab,\\qquad S(N)=a+b.\n\\]\nSubstitute these expressions into the given condition:\n\\[\n10a+b=ab+a+b.\n\\]\nSubtracting \\(a+b\\) from both sides gives \\(9a=ab\\). Since a two-digit number has \\(a\\ne0\\), division by \\(a\\) is valid and gives \\(b=9\\).\n\nThis digit works for every allowed \\(a\\), because \\(9a+(a+9)=10a+9\\). Thus the units digit is \\(\\boxed{9}\\), choice E.","solutionAvailability":"present","solutionMethods":[{"directNodeIds":["cnm_p017","cnm_027","cnm_040"],"explanation":"Let \\(a\\) be the tens digit and \\(b\\) the units digit. Then \\(1\\le a\\le9\\), \\(0\\le b\\le9\\), and\n\\[\nN=10a+b,\\qquad P(N)=ab,\\qquad S(N)=a+b.\n\\]\nSubstitute these expressions into the given condition:\n\\[\n10a+b=ab+a+b.\n\\]\nSubtracting \\(a+b\\) from both sides gives \\(9a=ab\\). Since a two-digit number has \\(a\\ne0\\), division by \\(a\\) is valid and gives \\(b=9\\).\n\nThis digit works for every allowed \\(a\\), because \\(9a+(a+9)=10a+9\\). Thus the units digit is \\(\\boxed{9}\\), choice E.","id":"model-tens-and-units","label":"Use tens and units","legacyRouteId":"model-tens-and-units","provenance":{"evidence":[{"label":"Accepted primary worked-solution review","path":"../database/knowledge-graph/evidence/solutions-2026-11-06/2001_AMC_10_Q06.json","sha256":"b5c280d746c3a3e60a786a97ec0b3e91f32eb2872e8d40e329e8dde033203806"}],"license":"CC-BY-NC-SA-4.0","licenseURL":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/","reviewStatus":"AI-authored and independently AI-reviewed; not teacher reviewed","source":"Original Math Practice solution","sourceRef":"amc/2001_AMC_10_Q06#model-tens-and-units","sourceURL":"https://github.com/rqzhu-aide/math-practice"}},{"directNodeIds":["cnm_040"],"id":"cancel-nonzero-tens-digit","label":"Cancel the tens digit","legacyRouteId":"cancel-nonzero-tens-digit","provenance":{"evidence":[{"label":"Legacy graph and grading review","path":"../database/knowledge-graph/evidence/solutions-2026-11-06/2001_AMC_10_Q06.json","sha256":"b5c280d746c3a3e60a786a97ec0b3e91f32eb2872e8d40e329e8dde033203806"}],"license":"CC-BY-NC-SA-4.0","licenseURL":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","source":"Existing reviewed concept route","sourceRef":"amc/2001_AMC_10_Q06#cancel-nonzero-tens-digit"}},{"directNodeIds":["cnm_151"],"id":"fac